;; =============================================================================
;; Spec: Ariadne step 2c — transacted effects (propose -> commit -> reconcile)
;; =============================================================================
;;
;; Normative refs: docs/architecture/rfc-ariadne-adoption.md (adoption
;; step 2; T3 contradiction 7 — "WorktreeInbox dead-drop measures
;; writes, not reads"); Ariadne §13.5 (transacted effects, unknown
;; outcome as a first-class state) + the delivery-is-not-acceptance
;; rule (Sneaky Bob, explainer #562).

{:spec/title "Ariadne step 2c: effect-transaction component - propose, commit, reconcile"

 :spec/description
 "Create component effect-transaction: the coordinator that turns an
  irreversible effect into a transaction with a durable record written
  BEFORE the effect and a reconciliation pass that discovers the true
  outcome afterwards.

  WHY. Today's irreversible effects execute directly and report
  outcomes they cannot substantiate. Source-verified:

    * `pr-lifecycle/merge.clj` `merge-pr!` builds
      [\"gh\" \"pr\" \"merge\" n method-flag \"--auto\"] - the --auto flag
      is UNCONDITIONAL - then returns {:merged? true} on exit 0. With
      --auto, exit 0 means 'auto-merge was ENABLED', not 'merged'. The
      published merged event then carries a SHA from
      `git rev-parse HEAD` in the LOCAL worktree - the branch tip, not
      the squash commit on base. Two claims the code cannot support.
    * `llm/protocols/impl/llm_client.clj` `handle-streaming` computes
      cost only on exit-code 0. A call that dies mid-stream or exits
      non-zero records NO COST - money spent, ledger empty.
    * `phase-deployment/deploy.clj` `enter-deploy` captures
      `capture-current-state` rollback evidence, then attaches it to
      ctx only on SUCCESS; the `store-apply-failure` branch DROPS it -
      the one artifact needed to undo a partial apply is discarded
      exactly when the apply failed.
    * `pr-lifecycle/events.clj` `publish!` is a swap! on an in-memory
      atom. Nothing about a merge attempt survives process death.

  THE REFERENCE IMPLEMENTATION ALREADY EXISTS IN-TREE. The RFC lists
  'makes Phase D commands transacted' as step-2 work, but the operator
  intervention path is ALREADY transacted and is the model to copy
  (source-verified): `event-stream/operator_requests.clj`
  `request-intervention!` lands the request with ATOMIC_MOVE and
  refuses to report success for a gesture that did not reach disk;
  `operator/consumer.clj` `route-intervention!` rebuilds the record
  server-side rather than trusting the client; `operator/application.clj`
  `apply-intervention!` advances to :dispatched BEFORE firing the
  effect, and `verify-readback!` compares {:observed :expected},
  transitioning to :verified only on match and to
  :control-state-readback-mismatch otherwise.

  So this spec GENERALIZES an existing working pattern rather than
  inventing one. That is a material scope reduction against the RFC's
  assumption.

  GROUP 1: The transaction record
  A closed Malli map, durable before the effect:
    :effect/id, :effect/class (the :grant/effect-class enum),
    :effect/grant-id (the grant authorizing it),
    :effect/envelope-id (the DecisionEnvelope that allowed it),
    :effect/proposal (the intended action, structured),
    :effect/state [:enum :proposed :committing :succeeded :failed
                   :unknown-outcome :reconciled],
    :effect/observed (readback result), :effect/at.

  GROUP 2: :unknown-outcome is first-class, not an error
  The state a process death between commit and confirmation lands in.
  It is NOT :failed - claiming failure for a merge that actually landed
  is as wrong as claiming success. Reconciliation resolves it; until
  then it is honestly unknown.

  GROUP 3: Durability
  Records land with the same ATOMIC_MOVE discipline as
  `operator_requests.clj` `write-event-file!`. A transaction that did
  not reach disk MUST NOT report a committed effect - the intervention
  path's stated rule, applied to effects.

  GROUP 4: Reconcile reads the world, not the log
  `reconcile!` takes a record in :unknown-outcome or :committing and
  asks the EXTERNAL system what actually happened (for merge: gh pr
  view --json state,mergeCommit). The answer, not the local guess,
  becomes :effect/observed. Mismatch between proposal and observation
  is recorded, never smoothed over.

  GROUP 5: Commit re-checks the grant
  Calls 2b's re-check entry point. A grant revoked or expired between
  propose and commit fails the commit and leaves the record honest.

  This spec builds the COORDINATOR ONLY - no call site is migrated
  here. 2d does that."

 :spec/intent {:type :feature
               :scope ["components/effect-transaction/"
                       "projects/*/deps.edn"]}

 :spec/constraints
 ["Record is durable (ATOMIC_MOVE) before the effect fires - not after, not in an atom"
  ":unknown-outcome is a distinct state from :failed; conflating them is a review rejection"
  "reconcile! MUST query the external system - it MUST NOT infer outcome from local state"
  "Commit re-checks the grant via the 2b entry point"
  "Follow the operator intervention lifecycle's shape; do not invent a second, divergent state machine"
  "Stratified design, max 3 layers per file"
  "New component MUST be added to all four projects' deps.edn or poly check fails"]

 :spec/acceptance-criteria
 ["A proposal record is readable from disk after a simulated process kill between propose and commit"
  "An effect interrupted after commit lands in :unknown-outcome, NOT :failed"
  "reconcile! on an :unknown-outcome merge record queries the external system and records the observed state"
  "reconcile! records a mismatch when the observed outcome contradicts the proposal, rather than overwriting the proposal"
  "Commit fails when the authorizing grant was revoked after the proposal was written"
  "A transaction whose record could not be written reports failure and does NOT fire the effect"
  "poly check reports 0 errors"
  "full bb test green"]

 :spec/priority
 {:tier :blocker
  :axes #{:correctness :observation :governance-credibility}
  :theme :ariadne-grants
  :rationale "The call-site migration in 2d has nothing to migrate onto until the coordinator exists."
  :blocks ["ariadne-merge-grant-enforcement.spec.edn"
           "ariadne-deploy-grant-enforcement.spec.edn"]
  :blocked-by ["ariadne-grant-constraints-decide.spec.edn"]}

 :spec/workflow-type :canonical-sdlc}
